
                            A m i N e t X D u o

              A TCP/IP stack for AmigaOS, on Eclipse ThreadX NetX Duo


WHAT IT IS
----------

AmiNetXDuo gives AmigaOS the bsdsocket.library that every Amiga networking
program already speaks.  It reaches the network card through the SANA-II
driver that every Amiga network card already has.  It is a complete stack,
not a shim: DHCP, ARP, ICMP, TCP, UDP and DNS.  The IPv6 build adds
neighbour discovery, duplicate address detection and stateless
autoconfiguration.

It is MIT licensed throughout, with no 4.4BSD or GPL lineage anywhere in it.


BEFORE THE INSTALLATION
-----------------------

This machine needs:

  *  AmigaOS 2.04 or newer.
  *  Any 68000 or better.  Encrypted (https:) connections work on every
     processor, the 68000 included, but a first handshake there takes a
     minute or two.  A resumed one takes under a second.
  *  1 MB of memory.  The stack takes about 520 KB of that once it is up.
     It sizes its packet buffers from what is left, so more memory means
     more buffers.  Encryption adds about 210 KB the first time it is used.
  *  A network card, and the SANA-II driver for that card.  The driver is a
     file whose name ends in ".device", and it came with the card.  Put the
     driver in DEVS: before the installation.  The installer then finds it
     and pre-selects the card.

Cards known to work this way, and the driver each one uses:

     Commodore A2065 . . . . . . . . . a2065.device
     Village Tronic Ariadne  . . . . . ariadne.device
     Village Tronic Ariadne II . . . . ariadne2.device
     Hydra Systems AmigaNet  . . . . . amiganet.device
     Individual Computers X-Surf . . . xsurf.device
     Individual Computers X-Surf 100 . xsurf100.device
     PCMCIA Ethernet (A600/A1200)  . . cnet.device
     3Com EtherLink III / 3C589  . . . etherlink3.device
     Prism2 wireless PCMCIA  . . . . . prism2.device

Any other SANA-II driver works too.  The last menu entry of the installer
takes the name of the driver.


INSTALLING
----------

Double-click "Install-AmiNetXDuo".

The default tool of its icon is "Installer", so Commodore's Installer must
be somewhere in the command path.  On a standard Workbench 3.1 it is, on the
Install disk and usually copied to C: or SYS:Utilities.  If Workbench cannot
find "Installer", set the Default Tool of the icon (Workbench menu:
Icons/Information) to the location of the copy on this machine.

From a Shell, the equivalent is:

     Stack 10000
     CD <the drawer this file is in>
     Installer SCRIPT Install-AmiNetXDuo APPNAME AmiNetXDuo

(Separate the keyword and the value with a space, not with "=".  Installer
2.17, the one on the Workbench 3.1 Install disk, parses its command line by
hand and does not recognise "SCRIPT=name".  The CD matters too: started from
a Shell, the script finds its files relative to the current directory.)

The installer asks which network card this machine has, and whether the
network hands out addresses automatically.  If the answer to the second
question is not known, answer yes.  Nearly every home network hands out
addresses, and the change afterwards is one line in one file.

It puts

     bsdsocket.library, usergroup.library,
     tls.library                               into LIBS:
     NetSetup, AddNetInterface, RemoveNetInterface,
     ConfigureNetInterface, Online, Offline,
     ShowNetStatus, ShowNetServices, GetNetStatus,
     CheckNetConfig, CheckNetDevice, NetShutdown,
     AddNetRoute, DeleteNetRoute, hostname,
     ping, netstat, host, nslookup, arp,
     fetch, nc, telnet, tftp, sntp, traceroute,
     whois, ssh, scp, httpd, iperf,
     NetCapture, NetTrace                      into C:
     the interface description                 into DEVS:NetInterfaces
     the routing and name server files         into DEVS:Internet
     this file, the documentation, examples    into a selected drawer

and, when it is allowed to, one line into S:User-Startup so the network
comes up when the machine boots.

tls.library is what makes encrypted (https:) connections work.  It is in the
archive only when the archive was built with encryption in it, and the
minimal stack leaves it out.  Its trust store goes to
DEVS:Internet/certificates.

Devs/Networks holds anxnet.device, AmiNetXDuo's own SANA-II driver, which
drives ten cards and replaces the driver that came with them.  The installer
ASKS whether to put it in DEVS:Networks and offers yes; copying it changes
nothing on its own, because nothing reads it until an interface file names
it.  To try it, put DEVICE=DEVS:Networks/anxnet.device in
DEVS:NetInterfaces/<name>, and add a CARD= line when the probe has to be told
which board it is.  CheckNetDevice reports what it found.  An anxnet.device
already on the machine is renamed to anxnet.device.old rather than being
overwritten, so the previous one can be put back.

Earlier releases did not offer it at all, so a machine kept whatever
anxnet.device it already had through every reinstall.

One thing in the archive is NOT installed, deliberately: the Developer
drawer holds headers, and where headers belong is a property of the compiler
rather than of this machine.

A second run of the installer over a working installation is safe.  It
notices the existing configuration and offers to leave it exactly as it is,
and it only ever replaces its own block in S:User-Startup.

There can already be a bsdsocket.library on the machine, from AmiTCP, Miami,
Genesis or Roadshow.  The installer then renames it to bsdsocket.library.old
rather than deleting it, and reports that it has done so.  Two TCP/IP stacks
cannot share one machine.


DID IT WORK?
------------

     ShowNetStatus              is it up, and what address did it get
     ping 192.168.1.1           can it reach the router
     host aminet.net            can it look a name up
     netstat -r                 what does it think the routes are

If ShowNetStatus reports that nothing is running, the usual reason is that
the driver of the card is not in DEVS:.  The second most usual reason is the
wrong card chosen in the installer.  Run the installer again and select
another card.


DOING IT BY HAND
----------------

Everything the installer writes is a plain text file that can be written by
hand.  There is a commented example of each of them in the Examples drawer:

     Examples/NetInterfaces/eth0        every keyword there is, explained
     Examples/NetInterfaces/a2065       the short version, one per card
     Examples/NetInterfaces/eth0-static a fixed address, worked through
     Examples/NetInterfaces/eth0-ipv6   IPv6 only, no IPv4 at all
     Examples/Internet/routes           the default route
     Examples/Internet/name_resolution  name servers and the host name
     Examples/Internet/hosts            names known without a name server

The minimum that works is three lines in DEVS:NetInterfaces/eth0:

     DEVICE=a2065.device
     UNIT=0
     CONFIGURE=DHCP

Note CONFIGURE, not IPTYPE.  Documentation elsewhere on the net that gives
IPTYPE=DHCP repeats an AmiTCP-era mistake: in Roadshow, IPTYPE is a number,
the SANA-II packet type.  AmiNetXDuo accepts both, so such a file still
works here.  It does not work on Roadshow.


USING SSH
---------

`ssh' is Dropbear's dbclient and `scp' is its traditional secure-copy client.
They need mathieeedoubbas.library in LIBS:,
which every Workbench installation has, and nothing else.  It brings its own
stack, so there is no `stack 200000' to type first.

     ssh me@192.168.1.10                    log in
     ssh me@192.168.1.10 "uname -a"         run one command
     scp -i DH0:keys/id_amiga RAM:test me@192.168.1.10:/tmp/test
                                                copy a file to the server
     scp -i DH0:keys/id_amiga me@192.168.1.10:/tmp/test RAM:test
                                                copy one back

This is the original SCP protocol, not SFTP.  The remote machine must have an
`scp' command; current OpenSSH installations normally retain one for this
compatibility mode.  Recursive copy (`scp -r') and timestamp preservation
(`scp -p') are supported.  Direct remote-to-remote and local-to-local copies
are not; use the ordinary `Copy' command for two local paths, or copy through
the Amiga in two commands for two remote paths.

`C:scp-runner' is scp's internal process helper.  It is not a command to run,
but it must remain in the same drawer as scp.

An Amiga volume or assign contains a colon, the same character SCP uses in
`host:path'.  Existing names such as `RAM:test' and `DH0:work/file' are
recognized as local.  `/DH0/work/new-file' is the unambiguous spelling when a
new path's volume or assign is not mounted yet.

A password works if the far end allows one.  A key works as well, and on a
machine this slow a key also saves the typing.

THE KEY MUST BE IN DROPBEAR'S FORMAT, not OpenSSH's.  An OpenSSH key copied
straight over is not read.  On the PC, with Dropbear's own tools:

     dropbearkey -t ed25519 -f id_amiga     make one
     dropbearconvert openssh dropbear ~/.ssh/id_ed25519 id_amiga
                                            or convert an existing one
     dropbearkey -y -f id_amiga             print the line for the server's
                                            authorized_keys

MAKE THE KEY ON THE PC, not on the Amiga.  This machine has very little
entropy to make one from, and ed25519 keygen on a 14 MHz 68020 takes about a
minute.  Copy the private key over afterwards.

NAME THE KEY WITH -i, every time:

     ssh -i DH0:keys/id_amiga me@192.168.1.10

There is a default path: dbclient reads ENV:HOME, appends /.ssh/id_dropbear,
and falls back to SYS: when ENV:HOME is unset.  A key in that place with -i
left off was tried and does NOT work.  The connection is refused with "No
auth methods could be used".  Use -i, and it works every time.

ENV:USER is the name sent when the command line has no user@ in front of the
host.  With none set it is "amiga".

     SetEnv SAVE USER me


WRITING PROGRAMS AGAINST IT
---------------------------

The Developer drawer holds the headers for what bsdsocket.library has that
the NDK does not declare: the RFC 3493 interface calls, if_nametoindex() and
its three companions, and the AF_INET6 names, from IPPROTO_IPV6 and
IPV6_V6ONLY to the IN6_IS_ADDR_* macros.  The installer does not copy that
drawer, because the location of headers is a property of the compiler rather
than of the machine.  Put it wherever the NDK is.  Developer/ReadMe explains
the rest, and Developer/examples holds two programs that work.

Nothing else needs it.  Everything the NDK already declares is reached the
way it always was.


UNINSTALLING
------------

     Delete LIBS:bsdsocket.library LIBS:usergroup.library
     Delete LIBS:tls.library
     Delete C:NetSetup C:AddNetInterface C:RemoveNetInterface
     Delete C:ConfigureNetInterface C:Online C:Offline
     Delete C:ShowNetStatus C:ShowNetServices C:GetNetStatus
     Delete C:CheckNetConfig C:CheckNetDevice C:NetShutdown
     Delete C:AddNetRoute C:DeleteNetRoute C:hostname
     Delete C:ping C:netstat C:host C:nslookup C:arp
     Delete C:fetch C:nc C:telnet C:tftp C:sntp C:traceroute
     Delete C:whois C:ssh C:scp C:scp-runner C:httpd C:iperf
     Delete C:NetCapture C:NetTrace
     Delete DEVS:NetInterfaces/eth0
     Delete DEVS:Internet/certificates
     Delete DEVS:Networks/anxnet.device
     Delete DEVS:Networks/anxnet.device.old

and take the ";BEGIN AmiNetXDuo" .. ";END AmiNetXDuo" block out of
S:User-Startup.  If another stack was installed before, rename
LIBS:bsdsocket.library.old back over LIBS:bsdsocket.library.

A minimal installation has no LIBS:tls.library and no
DEVS:Internet/certificates, and Delete says so; add QUIET to those two lines
to silence it.  DEVS:Networks/anxnet.device and, if an earlier install left
one, DEVS:Networks/anxnet.device.old go the same way.


STATUS
------

It gets a DHCP lease, answers ARP, pings its gateway, resolves DNS, moves
TCP in both directions and listens for incoming connections.
bsdsocket.library scores 142 of 142 on the bsdsocktest conformance suite.

Everything above was measured on an emulated 68000, 68020 and 68030 under
Kickstart 2.04 and 3.1.  It has also run on real hardware: an A3000 with
an X-Surf-100, where a user measured 795 KB/s.


LICENCE
-------

MIT.  ThreadX and NetX Duo are MIT as well (Microsoft / Eclipse ThreadX
contributors) and are used unmodified.

One file is not MIT.  DEVS:Internet/certificates, present only in an archive
that also carries tls.library, is the Mozilla root certificate set, which is
covered by the Mozilla Public License 2.0.  It is built, unmodified in
substance, from the dated snapshot at third_party/cacert/cacert.pem in the
source tree, itself a verbatim copy of https://curl.se/ca/cacert.pem.  The
converter is tools/mkcertstore.py.  MPL 2.0 is file-scoped, so nothing else
here is affected, and third_party/cacert/README.md sets the reasoning out in
full.

No AmiTCP, AROSTCP, Miami or Roadshow code is used, copied or disassembled.
Olaf Barthel's freely distributable Roadshow SDK headers and autodocs were
used as an ABI reference only.
